# Day 14 — Transaction Idempotency

## Goal

Prevent duplicate financial transactions when a client retries the same request because of:

* Network timeout
* Slow response
* Double-click
* Mobile retry
* Gateway retry
* Temporary connection failure

## Problem

Without idempotency, submitting the same request twice could create two transactions:

```text
Request 1 → Deposit $100 → Transaction 21
Request 2 → Deposit $100 → Transaction 22
```

This could result in duplicate money movement.

## Solution

The client sends a unique header:

```text
Idempotency-Key: deposit-account-21-001
```

The Transaction Service stores:

```text
IDEMPOTENCY_KEY
REQUEST_HASH
```

The request hash is generated using SHA-256 from the transaction details.

## Request flow

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A["Receive request and key"] --> B{"Key already exists?"}
    B -- No --> C["Validate accounts"]
    C --> D["Generate request hash"]
    D --> E["Save PENDING transaction"]
    B -- Yes --> F{"Request hash matches?"}
    F -- Yes --> G["Return existing transaction"]
    F -- No --> H["Return 409 TXN-004"]
```

## Database changes

Created Flyway migration:

```text
V2__add_transaction_idempotency.sql
```

Added columns:

```sql
IDEMPOTENCY_KEY VARCHAR2(100)
REQUEST_HASH VARCHAR2(64)
```

Added a unique constraint:

```sql
CONSTRAINT UK_TRANSACTIONS_IDEMPOTENCY_KEY
UNIQUE (IDEMPOTENCY_KEY)
```

The database constraint protects against concurrent requests reaching the service at the same time.

