```

## Behavior

| Scenario                       | Result                                   |
| ------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------- |
| Header missing                 | `400`, `VAL-002`                         |
| Header blank                   | `400`, `VAL-002`                         |
| Header exceeds 100 characters  | `400`, `VAL-002`                         |
| New key                        | Creates one `PENDING` transaction        |
| Same key and same request      | Returns existing transaction             |
| Same key and different request | `409`, `TXN-004`                         |
| Concurrent duplicate request   | Unique constraint prevents duplicate row |

## Swagger test

Endpoint:

```text
POST http://localhost:8082/api/v1/transactions
```

Header:

```text
Idempotency-Key: deposit-account-21-001
```

Body:

```json
{
  "transactionType": "DEPOSIT",
  "sourceAccountId": null,
  "targetAccountId": 21,
  "amount": 100.00,
  "currency": "USD",
  "description": "Test deposit"
}
```

The repeated request returned the same values:

```text
transactionId: 21
transactionReference: 572c27e1-ac11-4b41-b407-36f4477034dc
```

## Oracle verification

```sql
SELECT
    TRANSACTION_ID,
    TRANSACTION_REFERENCE,
    IDEMPOTENCY_KEY,
    REQUEST_HASH,
    AMOUNT,
    TRANSACTION_STATUS
FROM TRANSACTIONS
WHERE IDEMPOTENCY_KEY = 'deposit-account-21-001';
```

Verified:

* Only one database record was created.
* The idempotency key was stored.
* A 64-character SHA-256 hash was stored.
* The transaction remained `PENDING`.

## Important distinction

Idempotency currently prevents duplicate transaction records. It does not yet:

* Debit balances
* Credit receiving accounts
* Reverse failed transfers
* Publish Kafka events
* Process external-bank transactions

These will be added in the transaction-processing phase.

## Day 14 outcome

The Transaction Service now safely handles repeated client requests and prevents duplicate financial transaction creation.
